#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# floor

#% text
# `floor(x)` gives the greatest integer less than or equal to `x`.

#% text
# `floor(x, a)` gives the greatest multiple of `a` less than or equal to `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact integers, fractions, machine reals and balls give exact integers.

#% item
# A machine real gives the integer of its exact binary value.

#% item
# A complex number is floored in its real and imaginary parts separately.

#% item
# `floor(x, a)` is `a*floor(x/a)`.

#% item
# `floor` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
floor(2.7)
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# Negative numbers round down, away from 0:

#% cell
floor(-2.7)
#% output
#= -3

#% cell
floor(7/2)
#% output
#= 3

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Multiples of a step:

#% cell
floor(7, 3)
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
floor(2.7, 0.5)
#% output
#= 2.5

#% cell
floor(17/3, 1/4)
#% output
#= 11/2

#% text
# A ball:

#% cell
floor(n(pi, 30))
#% output
#= 3

#% text
# A complex number:

#% cell
floor(3 + 2.5im)
#% output
#= 3 + 2im

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
floor([1.5, -1.5, 2])
#% output
#= [1, -2, 2]

#% text
# A large machine real, exactly:

#% cell
floor(1e20)
#% output
#= 100000000000000000000

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The integer part of a number just below an integer, exactly:

#% cell
floor(1 - 10^-60)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `ceiling` rounds up, `floor` down:

#% cell
[floor(2.1), ceiling(2.1)]
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% text
# `mod` is defined through `floor`:

#% cell
17 - 5*floor(17/5) == mod(17, 5)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `ceiling`, `round`, `mod`, `quotient`
